# Design System

## Brand character

The site must communicate:
- premium produce
- freshness
- expertise
- confidence
- culinary sophistication
- natural quality
- dependable B2B supply

The design should feel editorial rather than template-driven.

## Layout philosophy

Use strong composition rather than decoration.

Prefer:
- generous whitespace
- large photography
- asymmetrical but controlled editorial grids
- clear content hierarchy
- intentional negative space
- strong alignment
- restrained borders and dividers

Avoid:
- every section becoming a centered card
- repeated identical card grids
- oversized rounded rectangles
- excessive section backgrounds
- decorative UI with no semantic purpose

## Typography

Use a deliberate typography hierarchy.

Recommended hierarchy:
- Display: expressive but highly legible
- H1: strong and concise
- H2: clear section statement
- H3: supporting structure
- Body: highly readable
- Eyebrow/label: restrained uppercase or small utility style

Keep headings short whenever possible.
Do not use oversized headings just to fill space.

## Color system

Base palette should remain restrained:
- warm ivory/off-white for main canvas
- deep botanical green for brand anchor
- charcoal for primary text
- muted neutrals for supporting UI
- restrained produce-inspired accent colors for emphasis only

Never create a rainbow interface because the catalogue contains colorful produce.

Do not hard-code arbitrary colors repeatedly. Centralize reusable values in theme tokens / CSS variables.

## Surfaces

Use mostly flat surfaces with subtle contrast.

Cards may use:
- soft borders
- subtle background shifts
- understated shadows only when necessary

Do not default to heavy shadows or floating glass panels.

## Buttons

Primary CTA:
- visually strong
- high contrast
- concise label
- obvious hover/focus state

Primary B2B CTA examples:
- Request a Quote
- Enquire Now
- Talk to Our Wholesale Team

Secondary actions should be visually quieter.

Avoid turning every link into a button.

## Imagery

Photography should carry visual emotion.
Prefer:
- macro produce
- clean product photography
- greenhouse/farm scenes
- chefs using ingredients
- professional produce handling
- finished dishes

Images should look intentional, high quality, and consistent.

Do not mix random image ratios throughout the site.
Define reusable image aspect-ratio variants for:
- hero
- product card
- product detail gallery
- article/recipe card

## Motion

Motion should be subtle and purposeful.
Use it for:
- page transitions where helpful
- image reveal
- hover affordances
- navigation state
- filter interaction

Avoid:
- excessive parallax
- constant movement
- bounce-heavy animations
- long entrance animations
- animation that delays content discovery

Respect reduced-motion preferences.

## Responsive composition

Do not simply stack desktop blocks vertically on mobile.
Re-compose sections when needed.

Mobile priorities:
1. Product clarity
2. Readable typography
3. Fast access to catalogue
4. Fast access to enquiry
5. Thumb-friendly controls

## Decorative rule

Every decorative visual must have a reason.
If removing it does not hurt communication, remove it.
